  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Elevated fire weather conditions today due to hot, dry, and \n   breezy conditions, especially in northwest Montana.\n\n-  Periods of smoke persists across the region today. Low storm\n   chances except over Lemhi County. \n\n-  Surge of monsoon moisture, aided by remnants of Hurricane\n   Elida, will bring increasing chances for wet thunderstorms\n   Tuesday through Thursday. Localized flash flooding is possible.\n\n-  Hot temperatures are expected this week, with highs reaching\n   90s up to 106 degrees in the lower elevations of Idaho. \n\n\nMostly dry conditions with periods of smoke and haze will persist\ntoday, originating from wildfires in Oregon and Washington. There\nis a low chance for isolated storms today into Monday, mainly\nconfined to Lemhi County. Monday night into Tuesday morning\nridge-level and gap easterly breezes will develop. These easterly\nwinds could help thin out some of the current smoke, or draw in\nnew smoke from Canada. \n\nA notable pattern shift begins later Monday with a push of monsoon\nmoisture, probably mostly in the form of cloud cover and scattered\nrain showers. Any rain showers could occur more over northwest\nMontana into Tuesday morning. Residual dry air in place may\nprevent too much accumulation. From Tuesday through Thursday, \nmore sunshine could allow for destabilization of the atmosphere \nfor showers and thunderstorms. With precipitable water values \napproaching one inch, which equates to around 200% of normal for \nthis time of year, any storms could produce heavy rainfall. The \nridge axis will be generally right on top of us which could work \nagainst experiencing widespread shower activity. With the rise of \ntemperatures getting back into the 90s to near 100 or above, those\nworking or recreating outdoors should stay hydrated, limit \nstrenuous outdoor activities during peak afternoon heating, and \nspend time in air conditioned or shaded environments. \n\nBy Friday into next weekend, the ridge axis is forecast to shift\neastwards, allowing for increasing westerly winds, drier\natmospheric moisture and a down tick in shower activity.",
